  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1150 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nToday through Tuesday: Dry with Increasing Temperatures\n\nSubtle ridging is forecast to move in over the region over the \ncoming days leading to mainly quiet weather across the Upper \nMidwest. This pattern will also aid in low level WAA through \nTuesday and temperatures each afternoon will be above average \nfor November in the mid to upper 50s to low 60s. Even though a \ncold front moves across the CWA this morning, the only \nnoticeable change will be the wind turning to out of the \nnorthwest. The CAA associated with the front should stay to the \nnorth and east over the Great Lakes. The lack of upper level \nforcing combined with a drier airmass in place should keep \nconditions dry through Tuesday.\n\nWednesday and Thursday: Light Rain Potential and a Cool Down.\n\nUpper level troughing, that is currently over the northeastern \nUS, will eventually have another wave that digs south over the \neastern US in the middle to end of this next work week. \nIncreasing mid level moisture out ahead of this feature should \nbe enough to lead to light precipitation potential, if synoptic \nlift moves over the Upper Mississippi River Valley. Where the \nbetter lift provided by the trough will be located varies quite \na bit in the latest suite of model guidance, thus the blended \nNBM PoPs remain low (10-25%) with this forecast cycle. If we do \nsee some precipitation with this system, it is highly likely to \nbe very light and the 90th percentile NBM rainfall values only \nup to 0.10 inches. If enough cold air can move south at the same\ntime as the light precipitation were to occur, cannot \ncompletely rule out some snow mixing in with rain, but that \npotential is very, very low. Cooler air will eventually move in \nover the area and temperatures should drop down to near average \non Wed/Thu. That trough will quickly depart off to the east by \nThursday afternoon and the axis of high pressure moves in behind\nthe trough.\n\nNext Weekend: High Pressure Sets In \n\nSurface high pressure originating out of the Canadian Prairie \nProvinces is expected to sink south into the Upper Midwest by \nFriday, dominating much of the weather pattern across the \nCentral CONUS by Saturday. This will keep conditions dry so if \nwe don't see any rain with Wednesday's activity, it will likely \nbe a dry forecast through next Sunday at least. Temperatures \nwill be at their coolest Thursday into Friday with some recovery\nexpected by Saturday into the 50s.",
